 \section*{Abstract}
 \addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}
 
-A digitally controlled USB-C laboratory supply was implemented using an inherited charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant topology and planar magnetics. Burst control was developed for secondary-bulk regulation, startup sequencing, and energy limitation at no load and after load removal.
+A digitally controlled USB-C power supply was implemented using an inherited charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant topology and planar magnetics. Burst control was developed for secondary-bulk regulation, startup sequencing, and energy limitation at no load and after load removal.
 
 The gallium-nitride (GaN) half bridge operated at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}. Average power was controlled by enabling bursts of switching cycles. A circular direct memory access (DMA) buffer supplied the feedback samples. Startup precharge and separate duty limits for loaded and no-load operation were implemented. The USB-C PD stage provided the tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings.
 
